Below is a **single‑file** C# console program that touches on most of the useful features of *tuples* in C#.  
It will compile with **C# 7.0+** (the default for .NET 6/7/8).  

```csharp
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// TupleDemo.cs
// ---------------------------------------------------------------

using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

class TupleDemo
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // 1. Creating a tuple with anonymous element names
        var person = ("Alice", 30);
        Console.WriteLine($"Person: {person}");           // prints (Alice, 30)

        // 2. Giving the tuple element names explicitly
        var namedPerson = (Name: "Bob", Age: 45);
        Console.WriteLine($"Named person: {namedPerson}"); // prints (Name: Bob, Age: 45)

        // 3. Deconstructing a tuple into local variables
        var (name, age) = person;
        Console.WriteLine($"Name: {name}, Age: {age}");    // prints Name: Alice, Age: 30

        // 4. Method returning a tuple
        var (sum, product) = CalculateSumAndProduct(3, 7);
        Console.WriteLine($"Sum: {sum}, Product: {product}"); // Sum: 10, Product: 21

        // 5. A list of tuples (e.g., points on a 2‑D plane)
        var points = new List<(double X, double Y)>
        {
            (0, 0),
            (3, 4),
            (5, 12)
        };

        // Calculate distances from the origin using LINQ and tuples
        var distances = points
            .Select(p => (Point: p, Distance: Math.Sqrt(p.X * p.X + p.Y * p.Y)))
            .OrderBy(t => t.Distance);

        Console.WriteLine("\nPoints sorted by distance from the origin:");
        foreach (var t in distances)
        {
            var p = t.Point;
            Console.WriteLine($"({p.X}, {p.Y})  Distance = {t.Distance:F2}");
        }

        // 6. Pattern matching with tuples (C# 7.0+)
        if (person is ("Alice", 30))
            Console.WriteLine("\nWe found Alice, age 30!");

        // 7. Nested tuples and deconstruction
        var nested = (Outer: (Inner: ("foo", 1), 42), Bar: 3.14);
        Console.WriteLine($"\nNested tuple: {nested}");

        var ((innerStr, innerNum), outerNum) = nested.Outer;
        Console.WriteLine($"Inner: ({innerStr}, {innerNum}), Outer: {outerNum}");

        // 8. Tuple equality and comparison
        var a = ("X", 10);
        var b = ("X", 10);
        Console.WriteLine($"\na == b? {a == b}"); // True – tuples compare element‑wise
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Example method that returns a tuple
    // ----------------------------------------------------------------
    static (int Sum, int Product) CalculateSumAndProduct(int a, int b)
    {
        return (a + b, a * b); // tuple literal
    }
}
```

### How to run

```bash
dotnet new console -n TupleDemoApp
# replace Program.cs with the code above
dotnet run
```

### What the program shows

| Feature | Code snippet | What you’ll see |
|---------|--------------|-----------------|
| Create tuple | `var person = ("Alice", 30);` | `(Alice, 30)` |
| Named elements | `var namedPerson = (Name: "Bob", Age: 45);` | `(Name: Bob, Age: 45)` |
| Deconstruct | `var (name, age) = person;` | `Name: Alice, Age: 30` |
| Return from method | `CalculateSumAndProduct(3, 7);` | `Sum: 10, Product: 21` |
| Tuple list & LINQ | `List<(double X, double Y)> points` | Sorted list of points by distance |
| Pattern matching | `if (person is ("Alice", 30))` | `We found Alice, age 30!` |
| Nested tuples | `var nested = (Outer: (Inner: ("foo", 1), 42), Bar: 3.14);` | Demonstrates nested deconstruction |
| Equality | `a == b` | `True` |

Feel free to drop in your own data or add more tuple operations—C# tuples are a lightweight, immutable way to bundle a handful of values together.
